ULTRALYTICS YOLO, AND THE POLO FORK -- INVOKED, AND OPTIONAL
============================================================

Ultralytics YOLO. Copyright (C) Ultralytics.
https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics

Dual-licensed: GNU Affero General Public License v3 or later, or a paid
Ultralytics Enterprise License that lifts the open-source requirements. Its
dependency ultralytics-thop carries the same AGPL v3 or later terms and is
named in no pyproject.toml, arriving only through Ultralytics itself.

POLO. https://github.com/mooch443/POLO

A third-party fork of Ultralytics YOLO that adds point detection, and ships
the AGPL v3 text. The Ultralytics Enterprise License is offered by
Ultralytics for its own distribution and does not extend to a third-party
fork, so POLO is AGPL-only.

Ultralytics reaches a user two of those ways at once -- invoked for
tracking, optional for training -- and which path a use takes decides what
that user owes.

Invoked, for tracking. `mosaic track ultralytics` drives Ultralytics as a
separate program in an environment the user builds. That environment's
definition is src/mosaic/tracking/external/ultralytics-env/, the program
that runs inside it is src/mosaic/tracking/external/runner/, and what
crosses between mosaic and that program is a JSON request file, a JSON
response file and progress lines on standard output. Mosaic locates the
environment through MOSAIC_ULTRALYTICS_CONDA_ENV or MOSAIC_ULTRALYTICS_BIN
and spawns its interpreter. On this path mosaic imports Ultralytics
nowhere, and no mosaic install carries it: the user builds the environment,
obtains Ultralytics from its own publisher under that publisher's terms,
and is the licensee.

One package by these authors is installed into that environment beside
Ultralytics: mosaic-media, under the Apache License 2.0, which the runner
reads video through. That is a licensing decision and not a packaging one.
Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with AGPL-3.0 -- it may be taken into an
AGPL-covered work, and the combining is done by the user who builds the
environment -- so it can sit there where mosaic-behavior, being
AGPL-3.0-or-later, cannot. mosaic-behavior is declared nowhere in that
environment and is not importable from it, and
tests/test_ultralytics_separation.py checks both.

That separation is a license requirement, not a packaging convenience. A
program that imports an AGPL-3.0 library is one work with it, so a mosaic
that imported Ultralytics could not be offered under terms of its own
without also licensing Ultralytics; two programs exchanging files are two
works. tests/test_ultralytics_separation.py holds that line in both
directions: no mosaic module outside the files it names may import
Ultralytics, and the runner program may not import mosaic.

Optional, for pose and point models. The separation covers tracking and
nothing else. Model training and single-model inference -- the train-pose,
train-points, infer-pose and infer-points ops, in
src/mosaic/tracking/pose_training/ -- still import Ultralytics inside
mosaic's own process. The [pose] extra installs upstream Ultralytics and
the [polo] extra installs the fork; [all] reaches [pose] by self-reference,
and so does [recommended], which is an alias for [all] kept for saved
install lines and removed in 0.13. A user who installs any of those four
has an AGPL-licensed dependency in their environment and runs a combined
work, exactly as before.

Obligation. AGPL v3 section 13 extends the duty to offer Corresponding
Source to anyone who interacts with the program remotely over a network, so
a commercial user is reached by operating a pipeline that includes
Ultralytics as a service, without ever redistributing a copy. Mosaic is
itself AGPL-3.0-or-later, so there is no incompatibility; the question is
whether a given use of the combined work can meet the AGPL's obligations.
For the [pose], [polo], [all] and [recommended] paths the options are to
comply, to buy an Enterprise License from Ultralytics for its own
distribution, or to install none of them. For the tracking environment the same choice is the user's
own, made where they build it; mosaic asks for nothing on their behalf.


THE ULTRALYTICS TRACKER DEFAULTS, AND THE METHODS THEY CONFIGURE
===============================================================

`mosaic track ultralytics` selects one of six tracker backends Ultralytics
ships. Mosaic records each backend's default settings in
src/mosaic/tracking/ultralytics_track/tracker_defaults.py, so that a run
identifier names the exact settings a run used and an upstream retune cannot
silently re-mint identifiers already on disk.

Those defaults are transcribed -- the setting names and their values, in
mosaic's own structure with mosaic's own commentary -- from the six files
ultralytics/cfg/trackers/{botsort,bytetrack,ocsort,deepocsort,fasttrack,
tracktrack}.yaml as of Ultralytics 8.4.63. The YAML files themselves are not
copied and no AGPL-licensed source is vendored into src/. A test reads the
tables back out of the Ultralytics environment and compares them against the
transcription, so drift fails by name.

The backends implement published methods. Results produced with one should
cite it:

  ByteTrack. Zhang, Y. et al. (2021). ByteTrack: Multi-Object Tracking by
  Associating Every Detection Box. arXiv:2110.06864.

  BoT-SORT. Aharon, N., Orfaig, R., Bobrovsky, B.-Z. (2022). BoT-SORT: Robust
  Associations Multi-Pedestrian Tracking. arXiv:2206.14651.

  OC-SORT. Cao, J. et al. (2022). Observation-Centric SORT: Rethinking SORT
  for Robust Multi-Object Tracking. arXiv:2203.14360.

  Deep OC-SORT. Maggiolino, G. et al. (2023). Deep OC-SORT: Multi-Pedestrian
  Tracking by Adaptive Re-Identification. arXiv:2302.11813.

  FastTracker. Hashempoor, H., Hwang, Y. D. (2025). FastTracker: Real-Time and
  Accurate Visual Tracking. arXiv:2508.14370.

  TrackTrack. Shim et al. (2025). Focusing on Tracks for Online Multi-Object
  Tracking. CVPR 2025.


